---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import data from './_data.json';

const { hang, flex, fg, grip, rec, trends, vol, derived } = data;
const T = Object.fromEntries(trends.map(([y, v]) => [String(y).slice(0, 4), v]));
const TR21 = T['2021'], TR22 = T['2022'], TR23 = T['2023'], TR24 = T['2024'], TR25 = T['2025'], TR26 = T['2026'];

const title = 'Dead Hang Statistics: Hang Times, Records and Grip Research';
const description = 'Measured dead hang times, Guinness hanging records, grip strength and mortality research, and search interest, with sources and a CSV download.';
const URL_ = 'https://deadhangs.com/dead-hang-statistics/';
const CSV = 'https://deadhangs.com/data/dead-hang-statistics.csv';

const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pct = (v: number, max: number) => Math.min(100, (v / max) * 100).toFixed(1);

const faqs: [string, string][] = [
  ['What is the average dead hang time?',
   'We found no published population average. One documented sample is GRIP-6: 25 recreational athletes with a mean age of 20.4 held a passive bar hang for a mean of 64.0 seconds (11 men) and 48.4 seconds (14 women) before training. Other small samples report 76.8 to 107.6 seconds under different protocols.'],
  ['What is the dead hang world record?',
   'Guinness lists Kenta Adachi of Japan at 80 minutes 41 seconds for the longest dead hang position, set on November 18, 2022. He also holds a separate title, hanging from a bar, at 4 hours 30 minutes 18 seconds from December 26, 2025. The two titles are different categories.'],
  ['Who holds the dead hang record for the oldest woman?',
   'Ann Crile Esselstyn of the USA, at 91 years 51 days, held a dead hang for 3 minutes 43 seconds in Cleveland, Ohio on September 7, 2026. Guinness requires at least 2 minutes for this record. She first set it at 90 years 231 days with 2 minutes 52 seconds on March 6, 2026.'],
  ['Is grip strength linked to how long people live?',
   'In large observational studies, lower grip strength was associated with a higher risk of death. In the PURE study, each 5 kg lower grip was associated with a hazard ratio of 1.16 for all-cause death. These are associations. They do not show that raising grip strength lengthens life.'],
  ['How many people search for dead hangs?',
   `Google Ads estimates about 18,100 US and 4,400 UK searches a month for "dead hang". Google Trends shows US interest in the term rising from an index of ${TR22} in 2022 to ${TR26} in January to October 2026. Both are estimates of relative or rounded demand, not counts of people.`],
  ['How do I cite this page?',
   'Cite the page title, DeadHangs.com, the update date and the URL, and name the original study for any figure you quote. Each row in the CSV carries its source link. A ready-to-copy citation sits in the "How to cite this page" section.'],
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map(([q, a]) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })),
};
const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: title,
  description,
  mainEntityOfPage: URL_,
  datePublished: '2026-11-02',
  dateModified: '2026-11-02',
};
const datasetSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Dead hang statistics: measured hang times, hanging records, grip research and search interest',
  description: `A compiled table of ${data.csv_rows} sourced rows: published bar-hang and bent-arm-hang samples, Guinness hanging records with dates, grip strength and mortality hazard ratios, and estimated search interest. Each row has a source link, period and sample.`,
  url: URL_,
  datePublished: '2026-11-02',
  dateModified: '2026-11-02',
  creator: { '@id': 'https://deadhangs.com/#organization' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://deadhangs.com/#organization' },
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  keywords: ['dead hang', 'bar hang time', 'grip strength', 'Guinness World Records', 'flexed arm hang'],
  distribution: [{ '@type': 'DataDownload', encodingFormat: 'text/csv', contentUrl: CSV }],
};
const schema =
  '<script type="application/ld+json">' + JSON.stringify(articleSchema) + '</script>' +
  '<script type="application/ld+json">' + JSON.stringify(datasetSchema) + '</script>' +
  '<script type="application/ld+json">' + JSON.stringify(faqSchema) + '</script>';

const chartRows = [
  { label: 'GRIP-6 women', v: 48.4 },
  { label: 'GRIP-6 men', v: 64.0 },
  { label: 'Climbers, porch edge', v: 76.8 },
  { label: 'Drury, standard bar', v: 95.4 },
  { label: 'Drury, Tri-Bar', v: 107.6 },
];
const CMAX = 120;
const trendMax = 100;
const volMax = 18100;
---
<Article h1={"Dead hang statistics: measured hang times, records and grip research"} subtitle={"Every measured bar-hang sample we could find, the Guinness hanging record timeline, grip strength and health research, and search interest, each with its source, period and sample. Download the CSV."} crumbs={[{"name":"Dead hang statistics"}]} updated={"Updated November 2, 2026"} title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
<div class="article-body dhs">

  <div class="callout" id="key-numbers">
    <p><strong>Key numbers</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Measured hang time:</strong> men 64.0 s (n = 11) and women 48.4 s (n = 14), mean passive bar hang before training. Source: GRIP-6, Chomiuk et al., 2025. Not a population norm.</li>
      <li><strong>Longest dead hang position:</strong> 80 min 41 s, Kenta Adachi, set November 18, 2022. Source: Guinness World Records.</li>
      <li><strong>Grip and mortality:</strong> hazard ratio 1.16 per 5 kg lower grip in 139,691 adults. Source: PURE, Leong et al., Lancet 2015. An association only.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="callout" id="glance">
    <h2 style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2>
    <ol class="stop-steps">
      <li><strong>We found no population norm.</strong> The measured bar-hang samples are small: 11 men averaged 64.0 s and 14 women 48.4 s in GRIP-6 (whole-sample mean age 20.4).</li>
      <li><strong>Protocols differ.</strong> Hang-to-failure means run from 48.4 s to 107.6 s across samples of 11 to 35 people under different rules, so no single "average" can be quoted.</li>
      <li><strong>The record is 80 min 41 s.</strong> Kenta Adachi, November 18, 2022, Guinness dead hang position, {fmt(derived.adachi_dh)} seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Grip links to mortality, as association.</strong> Each 5 kg lower grip came with a 1.16 hazard ratio for all-cause death in PURE (139,691 adults).</li>
      <li><strong>Interest has grown.</strong> US Google Trends index for "dead hang" rose from {TR22} (2022) to {TR26} (January to October 2026).</li>
    </ol>
    <p><a class="dh-btn" href="/data/dead-hang-statistics.csv" download>Download the CSV ({data.csv_rows} rows)</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="stat-row" role="list">
    <div class="stat-card" role="listitem"><div class="stat-value">64.0 s</div><div class="stat-label">Men, mean bar hang (n = 11)</div><div class="stat-source">GRIP-6, 2025</div></div>
    <div class="stat-card" role="listitem"><div class="stat-value">48.4 s</div><div class="stat-label">Women, mean bar hang (n = 14)</div><div class="stat-source">GRIP-6, 2025</div></div>
    <div class="stat-card" role="listitem"><div class="stat-value">80:41</div><div class="stat-label">Longest dead hang position</div><div class="stat-source">Guinness, 2022</div></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="hang-times">How long can people hang from a bar? Measured samples</h2>
  <p>Published bar-hang samples are few and small. The table lists every hang-to-failure dataset with a reported mean in seconds that we could open: GRIP-6 (25 people, mean age 20.4, passive bar hang), a 2004 sample of 32 men (straight-arm bar hang), and 35 climbers measured before a 24-hour event (porch-edge hang, not a bar). None is a population norm.</p>

  <p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Measured hang-to-failure datasets">
    <table>
      <caption>Hang-to-failure datasets: sample, protocol and result. Seconds are means unless noted. Source links open the original paper.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dataset</th><th scope="col">Sample</th><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Protocol</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {hang.map((h) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{h.dataset}</th>
            <td>n = {h.n}; {h.sex}</td>
            <td>{h.age}</td>
            <td>{h.protocol}</td>
            <td><strong>{h.result}</strong></td>
            <td><a href={h.url} rel="noopener">{h.source}</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <figure class="dhs-fig">
    <div class="dhs-bars" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart of mean hang-to-failure time in seconds: GRIP-6 women 48.4, GRIP-6 men 64.0, climbers (porch-edge hang) before a 24-hour event 76.8, Drury standard bar 95.4, Drury Tri-Bar 107.6. The protocols differ, so the bars are not directly comparable.">
      {chartRows.map((r) => (
        <div class="dhs-row"><div class="dhs-label">{r.label}</div><div class="dhs-track"><div class="dhs-bar" style={`width:${pct(r.v, CMAX)}%`}><span>{r.v} s</span></div></div></div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption class="caption">Mean hang-to-failure time (seconds) in the samples above, on a 0 to 120 s scale. The bars come from different people, bars and rules, so they show the spread between studies, not a ranking. Sources: Chomiuk et al. 2025, Yu et al. 2023, Drury et al. 2004.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="note">Spread is large inside every sample: the standard deviation is 29 to 35 seconds in GRIP-6 and 33 seconds in the climbers, roughly 40 to 60% of the mean. The GRIP-6 paper prints the whole-sample before SD as 3.2 s, which does not fit its subgroup SDs, so this page reports no whole-sample before SD. In GRIP-6 the mean rose from 55.3 s to 60.9 s over 6 weeks of gym and climbing training (change 5.6 s, 95% CI 4.0 to 7.3). The same climbers averaged 76.8 s before and 38.1 s within an hour after 24 hours of climbing, a fatigue measurement.</p>

  <h2 id="published-targets">Where do published hang-time targets fit?</h2>
  <p>Published hang-time targets from fitness sites and podcasts are opinions, not measurements. They disagree with each other and name no sample of people hanging. The comparison table lives on one page so the numbers are kept in one place.</p>
  <p>See the <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/#published-targets">comparison of published hang-time targets</a> on the dead hang time by age page.</p>

  <h2 id="flexed">What are the bent-arm and flexed-arm hang numbers?</h2>
  <p>A flexed-arm or bent-arm hang is held with the elbows bent, not the arms straight. It is a different test from a dead hang, so its times must never be read as dead hang times. The two sources here are 88 university students and the FitnessGram school minimums.</p>
  <p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Bent-arm and flexed-arm hang datasets">
    <table>
      <caption>Bent-arm and flexed-arm hang figures. These are not dead hang times.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Dataset</th><th scope="col">Sample</th><th scope="col">Age</th><th scope="col">Protocol</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {flex.map((h) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{h.dataset}</th>
            <td>{h.n.startsWith('Criterion') ? h.n : `n = ${h.n}`}; {h.sex}</td>
            <td>{h.age}</td>
            <td>{h.protocol}</td>
            <td><strong>{h.result}</strong></td>
            <td><a href={h.url} rel="noopener">{h.source}</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">FitnessGram figures are minimums that define the Healthy Fitness Zone, not averages. The full age-by-age table, with all {fg.ages.length} age rows for boys and girls, is on the <a href="/flexed-arm-hang/">flexed arm hang norms page</a> and in the CSV download.</p>

  <h2 id="grip">What does research say about grip strength and health?</h2>
  <p>Large cohort studies found that lower grip strength was associated with higher mortality. In the PURE study each 5 kg lower grip came with a hazard ratio of 1.16 for all-cause death. These are observational associations and do not show that raising grip strength changes outcomes.</p>
  <p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Grip strength and health statistics">
    <table>
      <caption>Grip strength statistics: measure, figure, sample and source. Mortality rows are associations in observational data.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">Figure</th><th scope="col">Sample</th><th scope="col">Period</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {grip.map((g) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{g.measure}</th>
            <td><strong>{g.value}</strong></td>
            <td>{g.sample}</td>
            <td>{g.period}</td>
            <td><a href={g.url} rel="noopener">{g.source}</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">Hazard ratio (HR) of 1.16 means a 16% higher hazard of death per 5 kg lower grip over the follow-up, after adjustment, in that cohort. PURE reported no significant association between grip strength and incident diabetes, hospital admission for pneumonia or COPD, injury from a fall, or fracture. Grip here means peak squeezing force on a dynamometer, not hang time. For age-by-age grip centiles see <a href="/grip-strength-by-age/">grip strength by age</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="records">What are the hanging world records, and when were they set?</h2>
  <p>Guinness World Records lists the longest dead hang position at 80 minutes 41 seconds (Kenta Adachi, November 18, 2022). It also lists separate titles for hanging from a bar and for the oldest woman to hold a dead hang. The table below dates each result.</p>
  <p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Timeline of Guinness hanging records">
    <table>
      <caption>Guinness hanging records by date. Different titles are different tests and are not interchangeable.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Guinness title</th><th scope="col">Holder</th><th scope="col">Result</th><th scope="col">Where</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rec.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{r.datelabel}</th>
            <td>{r.title}</td>
            <td>{r.holder}</td>
            <td><strong>{r.result}</strong></td>
            <td>{r.where}</td>
            <td><a href={r.url} rel="noopener">{r.source}</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The dead hang position record went from 13:52 (2018) to 16:03 (2020) to 80:41 (2022). Adachi's 4,841 seconds is {derived.ratio_adachi_riise} times Riise's {derived.riise} seconds. His bar-hanging title, {fmt(derived.adachi_bar)} seconds, is {derived.ratio_bar_vs_dh} times his dead hang position time. Guinness says records change daily and are not always published online at once, and the record pages mostly do not state the rules of each title (the oldest-woman page states a 2-minute minimum). See the <a href="/dead-hang-world-record/">dead hang world record page</a> for the categories.</p>
  <p class="note">Guinness page text for the oldest-person (female) dead hang title shows Judis at 80 years 353 days (November 11, 2024) on a May 2025 archive copy. The March 31, 2026 news article gives her age as 81 and Esselstyn's first result as 2 minutes 52 seconds. We use the record-page figures where a page exists and the news article otherwise. We found no Guinness page for a female longest-duration dead hang position, so the female longest-duration title listed is hanging from a bar.</p>

  <h2 id="search">How much do people search for dead hangs?</h2>
  <p>US Google Trends interest in "dead hang" rose from an index of {TR22} in 2022 to {TR26} in January to October 2026, about {derived.trends_ratio} times higher and more than 50% above the same months of 2025. The 2021 figure covers only November and December. Trends values shift by a point or two between pulls (a second pull on November 2 gave about 59 for 2026). Google Ads estimates 18,100 US and 4,400 UK searches a month. Both measures are estimates, and they measure different things.</p>

  <figure class="dhs-fig">
    <div class="dhs-bars" role="img" aria-label={`Bar chart of the yearly average Google Trends index for dead hang in the US: 2021 (November to December only) ${TR21}, 2022 ${TR22}, 2023 ${TR23}, 2024 ${TR24}, 2025 ${TR25}, 2026 January to October ${TR26}.`}>
      {trends.map(([y, v]) => (
        <div class="dhs-row"><div class="dhs-label">{y}</div><div class="dhs-track"><div class="dhs-bar dhs-bar-b" style={`width:${pct(v as number, trendMax)}%`}><span>{v}</span></div></div></div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption class="caption">Google Trends relative interest in "dead hang", US, web search, yearly average of monthly index values (0 to 100 within the query set). Pulled November 1, 2026 for November 1, 2021 to October 31, 2026 via DataForSEO. The 2021 figure covers November and December only, and 2026 covers January to October. Relative interest, not search volume.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="table-cue">Scroll horizontally on a smaller screen.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Estimated monthly search volume, US and UK">
    <table>
      <caption>Estimated monthly searches (Google Ads, October 2025 to September 2026, pulled November 1, 2026 via DataForSEO). Estimates: Google rounds volumes and merges close variants.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Search term</th><th scope="col">US per month</th><th scope="col">UK per month</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {vol.map(([k, us, uk]) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{k}</th>
            <td>{fmt(us as number)}</td>
            <td>{fmt(uk as number)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cite">How to cite this page</h2>
  <p>Cite this page as a compiled table and name the original study for any single figure. Each row links to its source. The citation below is ready to copy, and the CSV carries the same rows with sample, period and source fields.</p>
  <div class="evidence-note">
    <p><strong>Citation.</strong> DeadHangs.com. "Dead Hang Statistics: Hang Times, Records and Grip Research." Updated November 2, 2026. {URL_}</p>
    <p><strong>Data.</strong> <a href="/data/dead-hang-statistics.csv" download>dead-hang-statistics.csv</a> ({data.csv_rows} rows). Columns: id, section, measure, group, sample_size, age, sex, protocol_or_context, value, spread, unit, period, source, source_url, note.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="method">How was this compiled?</h2>
  <p>Every figure comes from a source we opened: the PubMed or PMC record for studies, the Guinness record page or news article, or a Wayback copy of a Guinness page. Search figures are our own pulls from DataForSEO on November 1, 2026. Derived numbers are shown with their arithmetic.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Studies were found by Europe PMC searches (October to November 2026) for "bar hang", "dead hang", "hang test", "static hang", "flexed arm hang" and "bent arm hang". We kept samples that report a mean hang time in seconds with a described protocol.</li>
    <li>Hangboard and small-edge finger hangs are excluded. The climbers' porch-edge hang (Yu et al.) is kept because it is a timed hang to failure with feet off the ground, and it is labelled as an edge hang, not a bar hang.</li>
    <li>Two other papers with a bar-hang test (Brazilian highway police, 2020; Special Olympics athletes, 2012) give percentages or no seconds in their abstracts and have no open full text, so they are not included.</li>
    <li>Guinness figures were read from the record pages on November 2, 2026, and from archived copies for earlier holders.</li>
    <li>Search volume is a Google Ads estimate. Google Trends is an index of relative interest. Neither counts people.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">What do these statistics not show?</h2>
  <p>These statistics do not give a typical hang time for any age or sex. The hang samples are small, young or trained, and use different bars and rules. Observational grip studies show associations only, and a record is one person's best attempt.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Means from different protocols are not comparable. A hang from a power-rack bar, a porch edge and a pull-up bar load the hands differently.</li>
    <li>Bent-arm and flexed-arm hang times are a different test from the dead hang.</li>
    <li>Grip strength is dynamometer force, not hang time. No source here converts one into the other.</li>
    <li>Hazard ratios apply to the cohort and follow-up named. They are not personal risk estimates.</li>
    <li>Not yet found: a peer-reviewed table of dead hang time by age in the general population, and a military or occupational standard for a straight-arm hang.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="faq">Dead hang statistics: frequently asked questions</h2>
  {faqs.map(([q, a]) => (<div class="faq"><h3>{q}</h3><p>{a}</p></div>))}

  <div class="sources">
    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Chomiuk T et al. Impact of 6-week combined gym and climbing training on handgrip strength and arm size: GRIP-6 study. J Funct Morphol Kinesiol 2025. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/jfmk10040427" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.3390/jfmk10040427</a></li>
      <li>Drury DG, Faggiono H, Stuempfle KJ. An investigation of the tri-bar gripping system on isometric muscular endurance. J Strength Cond Res 2004;18(4):782-786. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1519/R-13883.1" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.1519/R-13883.1</a></li>
      <li>Yu E et al. Change in grip strength, hang time, and knot tying speed after 24 hours of endurance rock climbing. Front Sports Act Living 2023. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2023.1224581" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.3389/fspor.2023.1224581</a></li>
      <li>Cepková A et al. Cluster analysis as an effective tool for identifying physical fitness in students. Front Physiol 2025;16:1634125. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2025.1634125" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.3389/fphys.2025.1634125</a></li>
      <li>California Department of Education. 2011-12 Physical Fitness Test: FITNESSGRAM Healthy Fitness Zones. September 2011 (copy hosted by Transylvania County Schools). <a href="https://transylvania.schoolboard.net/sites/nc.transylvania.schoolboard.net/files/FitnessGram%20Healthy%20Fitness%20Zones.pdf" rel="noopener">PDF</a></li>
      <li>Leong DP et al. Prognostic value of grip strength: findings from the PURE study. Lancet 2015;386(9990):266-273. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(14)62000-6" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(14)62000-6</a></li>
      <li>Celis-Morales CA et al. Associations of grip strength with cardiovascular, respiratory, and cancer outcomes and all cause mortality. BMJ 2018;361:k1651. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k1651" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k1651</a></li>
      <li>Dodds RM et al. Grip strength across the life course: normative data from twelve British studies. PLOS ONE 2014;9(12):e113637. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0113637" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0113637</a></li>
      <li>Cruz-Jentoft AJ et al. Sarcopenia: revised European consensus on definition and diagnosis. Age Ageing 2019;48(1):16-31. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/ageing/afy169" rel="noopener">doi.org/10.1093/ageing/afy169</a></li>
      <li>Guinness World Records: <a href="https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/118967-longest-duration-in-the-dead-hang-position" rel="noopener">dead hang position</a>, <a href="https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/780102-longest-duration-hanging-from-a-bar-male" rel="noopener">hanging from a bar (male)</a>, <a href="https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/785984-longest-duration-hanging-from-a-bar-female" rel="noopener">hanging from a bar (female)</a>, <a href="https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/world-records/771587-oldest-person-to-hold-the-dead-hang-position-female" rel="noopener">oldest person, dead hang position (female)</a>, and the news articles of <a href="https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2021/6/norwegian-man-achieves-gruelling-16-minute-dead-hang-for-new-record-663582" rel="noopener">June 11, 2021</a> and <a href="https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2026/3/determined-90-year-old-grandma-breaks-record-for-oldest-woman-to-hold-a-dead-hang" rel="noopener">March 2026</a>.</li>
      <li>Search data: Google Trends and Google Ads search volume, retrieved via the DataForSEO API on November 1, 2026.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</div>
</Article>

<style is:global>
  .dhs .note { font-size: 14px; }
  .dhs .sources a, .dhs li, .dhs td, .dhs th { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .dhs table { min-width: 720px; }
  .dhs .table-scroll th[scope="row"] { text-align: left; }
